## Where Egypt began, every year.

Every summer, without fail, the Nile rose. It brought the black silt that fed the country, and if it rose too little there was hunger, too much and there was ruin. The Egyptians believed the flood welled up from caverns beneath the first cataract, right here, among the granite boulders of Aswan. So they raised their temples to the gods who kept it on the island in the middle of the river: Elephantine.

You arrive the way people always have, by boat. A felucca carries you across from the corniche, and in a few minutes you are stepping onto an island that has been lived on for some five thousand years.

## Satet, Khnum and the flood

Satet was the guardian of Egypt's southern border, the goddess who sent the flood downstream. Her sanctuary on Elephantine began as a shrine among the rocks and was rebuilt by king after king for three thousand years, each new temple on top of the last. Beside her stood Khnum, the ram-headed potter who shaped people on his wheel and was said to hold the Nile's source in his keeping. His great temple platform still looks out over the water.

Around them lie the layers of the town, the blocks of the rebuilt temple, and the inscriptions left by the officials who governed Egypt's south from here. On the Signature day, a licensed Egyptologist can join you to read them.

> They did not pray for the river to rise. They measured it, and they built their faith around the number.

## The steps into the river

Then the Nilometer. A stairway cut into the granite runs down into the Nile, its walls marked to read the height of the flood. The number set the year's taxes and told Egypt whether to expect plenty or famine. Standing on the steps with the water moving past below you, the whole logic of ancient Egypt suddenly makes sense.

## Left to us

The felucca, the tickets, the timing: your concierge takes care of it all, with local knowledge and our craft. Afterwards there is time to walk through the Nubian village among the palms, quietly and as guests, and lunch at a table we know. Pair it with Philae and you have the two great goddess temples of Aswan in a single unhurried day.
